.TH SLEEP 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sleep \- suspend execution for an interval
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sleep
.I seconds
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sleep
suspends execution for
.I seconds
seconds by calling
.BR sleep (3),
then exits.
.PP
.I seconds
is converted with
.BR atoi (3):
only a leading decimal integer is used, so a fractional part is
discarded and a suffix such as
.BR s ,
.B m
or
.B h
is ignored.
A value that does not begin with a number is taken as 0 and
.B sleep
returns at once.
Arguments after the first are ignored.
.PP
There are no options.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The interval elapsed (or was 0).
.TP
.B 1
No operand was given; a usage message is written to standard output.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
$ sleep 5; echo done
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sh (1),
.BR time (1)
.SH BUGS
Invalid operands are not diagnosed.
A negative value is passed to
.BR sleep (3)
as a huge unsigned number, so
.B "sleep \-1"
effectively sleeps forever.
.PP
The usage message goes to standard output instead of standard error.
